1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,580
This video, I'll continue
discussion about

2
00:00:02,580 --> 00:00:05,865
embedded testing and
talk about CI and CD.

3
00:00:05,865 --> 00:00:07,770
We're going to introduce CI, CD,

4
00:00:07,770 --> 00:00:10,215
continuous integration and
continuous deployment,

5
00:00:10,215 --> 00:00:13,095
and introduce a couple
of CI frameworks,

6
00:00:13,095 --> 00:00:15,940
Travis CI and GitLab CI.

7
00:00:16,460 --> 00:00:20,550
What is continuous integration,
continuous deployment?

8
00:00:20,550 --> 00:00:23,025
As I've mentioned in
the previous videos,

9
00:00:23,025 --> 00:00:25,305
when you're working with
multiple developers,

10
00:00:25,305 --> 00:00:28,275
you want to try to merge
as much as you can,

11
00:00:28,275 --> 00:00:30,255
as early as you can,

12
00:00:30,255 --> 00:00:32,700
and have shared
repository branches

13
00:00:32,700 --> 00:00:34,380
that everybody is
working off of.

14
00:00:34,380 --> 00:00:37,675
When that merge happens
to the shared branch,

15
00:00:37,675 --> 00:00:40,040
you want some kind of CI
pipeline that's going to help

16
00:00:40,040 --> 00:00:43,040
verify for you that the
build still succeeds,

17
00:00:43,040 --> 00:00:45,050
the automated tests
still succeeds.

18
00:00:45,050 --> 00:00:47,270
If you're working with
a hardware device,

19
00:00:47,270 --> 00:00:48,710
it'd be great to know
that the code can

20
00:00:48,710 --> 00:00:50,780
still be deployed
successfully to hardware.

21
00:00:50,780 --> 00:00:53,210
Then ideally there'd be
some level of automation on

22
00:00:53,210 --> 00:00:54,530
the hardware that you can run

23
00:00:54,530 --> 00:00:56,380
as well that still succeeds.

24
00:00:56,380 --> 00:00:58,890
This is like a
classic drawing of

25
00:00:58,890 --> 00:01:01,920
what a CI/CD system looks like.

26
00:01:01,920 --> 00:01:04,760
The version control
system can build in when

27
00:01:04,760 --> 00:01:07,895
there's a new commit or there's
new merged to a branch.

28
00:01:07,895 --> 00:01:09,905
There can be a build
that's kicked off,

29
00:01:09,905 --> 00:01:11,840
that can kick off a unit test,

30
00:01:11,840 --> 00:01:15,395
that can kick off a
deployment in our case,

31
00:01:15,395 --> 00:01:19,570
to hardware, an automated
test maybe at that point.

32
00:01:19,570 --> 00:01:23,210
Then if you're
working on a web app,

33
00:01:23,210 --> 00:01:25,460
you can even have the
deployment directly to

34
00:01:25,460 --> 00:01:28,830
production and have devices get

35
00:01:28,830 --> 00:01:32,705
deployed or have web app

36
00:01:32,705 --> 00:01:34,670
automatically be in the
pipeline for updates

37
00:01:34,670 --> 00:01:36,880
based on whether it's
passing test cases.

38
00:01:36,880 --> 00:01:38,900
In embedded devices,

39
00:01:38,900 --> 00:01:40,580
maybe that's a little
bit harder to do again

40
00:01:40,580 --> 00:01:44,030
because a lot of times there's
a manual part of testing

41
00:01:44,030 --> 00:01:45,650
that still needs to be
done before it's ready

42
00:01:45,650 --> 00:01:47,945
to deploy to production.

43
00:01:47,945 --> 00:01:52,340
You can often have everything
up to that point and

44
00:01:52,340 --> 00:01:55,640
possibly even some level of
deployment covered as a part

45
00:01:55,640 --> 00:02:00,460
of this production
feedback cycle from CI/CD.

46
00:02:00,460 --> 00:02:03,335
A couple of different
ways to do CI/CD.

47
00:02:03,335 --> 00:02:07,115
Jenkins is the traditional
way to do CI/CD.

48
00:02:07,115 --> 00:02:08,705
It's been around
for quite a while,

49
00:02:08,705 --> 00:02:10,850
originally created in 2004.

50
00:02:10,850 --> 00:02:13,520
It's an open-source
software solution

51
00:02:13,520 --> 00:02:15,200
that's intended to
be self-deployed.

52
00:02:15,200 --> 00:02:17,180
In other words, it
doesn't run in the cloud.

53
00:02:17,180 --> 00:02:18,500
It's something that you

54
00:02:18,500 --> 00:02:20,570
would typically deploy
on your own server.

55
00:02:20,570 --> 00:02:22,130
It's got a web UI
that you can use

56
00:02:22,130 --> 00:02:23,840
for configuration and setup.

57
00:02:23,840 --> 00:02:26,240
Then there's a text-based
Jenkins file that helps you

58
00:02:26,240 --> 00:02:28,910
define your pipeline
and by pipeline,

59
00:02:28,910 --> 00:02:30,200
meaning the connection to

60
00:02:30,200 --> 00:02:34,430
the repository that you're
using for source code control,

61
00:02:34,430 --> 00:02:36,875
the commands you use to
run your automated test,

62
00:02:36,875 --> 00:02:39,740
any commands you do for
deployment, et cetera.

63
00:02:39,740 --> 00:02:42,980
It's meant for doing
things like being able to

64
00:02:42,980 --> 00:02:46,000
deploy devices as a
part of the pipeline.

65
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:49,284
It's a great application for
device update for instance,

66
00:02:49,284 --> 00:02:50,870
in terms of what's covered in

67
00:02:50,870 --> 00:02:53,300
other videos for
updating devices.

68
00:02:53,300 --> 00:02:56,570
If you make automated
device update

69
00:02:56,570 --> 00:02:58,610
as a part of your
deployment process,

70
00:02:58,610 --> 00:03:02,700
then you can test it through
something like Jenkins.

71
00:03:03,430 --> 00:03:07,015
Jenkins again is the
classic way to do this.

72
00:03:07,015 --> 00:03:09,680
There are other and
newer options that have

73
00:03:09,680 --> 00:03:10,850
more tight integration with

74
00:03:10,850 --> 00:03:14,090
git revisions control
specifically.

75
00:03:14,090 --> 00:03:16,550
I probably wouldn't
choose Jenkins

76
00:03:16,550 --> 00:03:18,740
for a new project going forward,

77
00:03:18,740 --> 00:03:20,210
but it's possible
that you'll see it

78
00:03:20,210 --> 00:03:22,710
just based on its history.

79
00:03:22,990 --> 00:03:26,240
I wanted to talk about some
of the other options that are

80
00:03:26,240 --> 00:03:28,535
available and before I do that,

81
00:03:28,535 --> 00:03:31,490
I wanted to mention
YAML because YAML is

82
00:03:31,490 --> 00:03:34,520
used as a configuration
script language

83
00:03:34,520 --> 00:03:38,060
for some

84
00:03:38,060 --> 00:03:41,750
of the possible CI options
that we'll talk about.

85
00:03:41,750 --> 00:03:43,670
YAML stands for Yaml Ain't

86
00:03:43,670 --> 00:03:45,755
Markup Language or
recursive algorithm.

87
00:03:45,755 --> 00:03:48,590
It's similar to JSON,

88
00:03:48,590 --> 00:03:51,085
which is described
in the IoT lecture,

89
00:03:51,085 --> 00:03:55,640
except JSON is designed
for simplicity and

90
00:03:55,640 --> 00:03:58,670
generation parsing which makes

91
00:03:58,670 --> 00:04:00,485
it maybe slightly
less human-readable.

92
00:04:00,485 --> 00:04:01,700
Still pretty human-readable,

93
00:04:01,700 --> 00:04:05,900
but still has the quotes
and the colons and

94
00:04:05,900 --> 00:04:07,700
the brackets that need to be in

95
00:04:07,700 --> 00:04:09,260
specific spots so maybe a

96
00:04:09,260 --> 00:04:11,275
little bit less human-readable,

97
00:04:11,275 --> 00:04:13,350
but YAML is the opposite.

98
00:04:13,350 --> 00:04:15,710
It prioritizes human
readability and

99
00:04:15,710 --> 00:04:18,500
also support for serializing
native data structures.

100
00:04:18,500 --> 00:04:20,900
In other words, taking
a data structure

101
00:04:20,900 --> 00:04:24,740
that's meant to or
that's implemented

102
00:04:24,740 --> 00:04:26,390
in something like C
code and being able

103
00:04:26,390 --> 00:04:28,130
to directly translate that into

104
00:04:28,130 --> 00:04:29,780
something that is documented in

105
00:04:29,780 --> 00:04:32,365
this easily human-readable form.

106
00:04:32,365 --> 00:04:34,895
It was really designed
as a replacement for

107
00:04:34,895 --> 00:04:37,535
Extensible Markup
Language or XML.

108
00:04:37,535 --> 00:04:40,685
This shows you the same
content representative,

109
00:04:40,685 --> 00:04:42,440
either in JSON or YAML,

110
00:04:42,440 --> 00:04:44,920
and you can see maybe
this YAML representation

111
00:04:44,920 --> 00:04:46,595
is just slightly more

112
00:04:46,595 --> 00:04:49,220
typical of what you'd
expect to read if you were

113
00:04:49,220 --> 00:04:51,965
just typing a note to
someone in a text file.

114
00:04:51,965 --> 00:04:54,770
This looks a little bit more
contrived and specifically

115
00:04:54,770 --> 00:04:58,620
formatted for easier
parsing by devices.

116
00:04:59,810 --> 00:05:03,790
Talking about some
specific CI frameworks,

117
00:05:03,790 --> 00:05:05,800
one of them you might
have noticed that's

118
00:05:05,800 --> 00:05:08,080
used for some of

119
00:05:08,080 --> 00:05:13,540
the components for the
ASD course is Travis CI.

120
00:05:13,540 --> 00:05:17,530
Travis CI is a hosted CI service

121
00:05:17,530 --> 00:05:19,570
that integrates really
well on GitHub.

122
00:05:19,570 --> 00:05:21,819
It's free for Open
Source Repositories,

123
00:05:21,819 --> 00:05:24,850
although I've found out
myself that it can't handle

124
00:05:24,850 --> 00:05:26,275
the Yocto or Buildroot builds

125
00:05:26,275 --> 00:05:28,445
even for Open Source
Repositories.

126
00:05:28,445 --> 00:05:32,070
It's controlled by
a simple YAML file,

127
00:05:32,070 --> 00:05:34,930
and here's the example of
a really simple case that

128
00:05:34,930 --> 00:05:37,480
I am just compiling

129
00:05:37,480 --> 00:05:40,345
a C file and running
a test at SH scripts.

130
00:05:40,345 --> 00:05:42,770
You can see it's pretty
easy to set up here

131
00:05:42,770 --> 00:05:45,080
in terms of having
a script that can

132
00:05:45,080 --> 00:05:47,570
just run simple commands and

133
00:05:47,570 --> 00:05:51,330
do simple test space
on any commit.

134
00:05:51,330 --> 00:05:53,140
The language specifies

135
00:05:53,140 --> 00:05:54,910
the program language
and that helps

136
00:05:54,910 --> 00:05:58,105
determine how the automated
tests will be run.

137
00:05:58,105 --> 00:06:01,180
The script line tells it
what to run when testing.

138
00:06:01,180 --> 00:06:02,800
You just need to
write that script so

139
00:06:02,800 --> 00:06:04,610
that it gives you
a non-zero return

140
00:06:04,610 --> 00:06:06,220
when there's an error and

141
00:06:06,220 --> 00:06:08,530
everything else will be
handled automatically for you.

142
00:06:08,530 --> 00:06:09,910
There's some links
here if you want to

143
00:06:09,910 --> 00:06:11,260
see an example on implementation

144
00:06:11,260 --> 00:06:14,765
of Travis-ci, use for AESD.

145
00:06:14,765 --> 00:06:16,945
The general steps to do this,

146
00:06:16,945 --> 00:06:18,460
you set up an account on

147
00:06:18,460 --> 00:06:20,515
Travis-ci and link
your GitHub account.

148
00:06:20,515 --> 00:06:22,945
Make sure that your
repository is public so that

149
00:06:22,945 --> 00:06:25,810
you can use the free option.

150
00:06:25,810 --> 00:06:28,765
Then you can view all your
builds on your dashboard.

151
00:06:28,765 --> 00:06:30,235
Here's an example that shows a

152
00:06:30,235 --> 00:06:32,455
specific commit that I did,

153
00:06:32,455 --> 00:06:35,875
that passed, tells
me how long it ran,

154
00:06:35,875 --> 00:06:37,900
tells me where it ran.

155
00:06:37,900 --> 00:06:42,020
This has some details about
the execution environment.

156
00:06:43,530 --> 00:06:46,660
If you look at the details
of your environment,

157
00:06:46,660 --> 00:06:49,210
you can see the exact output
that you would have seen

158
00:06:49,210 --> 00:06:52,910
if you ran that on
your local system.

159
00:06:54,360 --> 00:06:57,820
Every time you do a commit
once you set it up,

160
00:06:57,820 --> 00:07:03,910
the pipeline and Travis-ci
will automatically

161
00:07:03,910 --> 00:07:06,730
re-run tests on every
commit and let you know

162
00:07:06,730 --> 00:07:10,240
if something failed with
messages like this.

163
00:07:10,240 --> 00:07:12,980
You can configure
it for emails, etc.

164
00:07:14,340 --> 00:07:16,690
You've probably
noticed these badges

165
00:07:16,690 --> 00:07:18,280
on other repositories.

166
00:07:18,280 --> 00:07:19,720
There's a badge that's generated

167
00:07:19,720 --> 00:07:21,940
for your project that
you can link from your

168
00:07:21,940 --> 00:07:24,880
Readme.markdown file
using something

169
00:07:24,880 --> 00:07:26,015
like this and there's

170
00:07:26,015 --> 00:07:28,420
a details page on
Travis-ci where you

171
00:07:28,420 --> 00:07:31,205
can see that this is the case.

172
00:07:31,205 --> 00:07:33,550
Let someone just see at

173
00:07:33,550 --> 00:07:35,710
a glance that you have
automated testing setup for

174
00:07:35,710 --> 00:07:38,410
your Build and verify
that the latest check-in

175
00:07:38,410 --> 00:07:42,200
on that branch has a
successful build result.

176
00:07:42,750 --> 00:07:45,430
A second CI framework
that I wanted

177
00:07:45,430 --> 00:07:47,855
to mention here is GitLab-CI.

178
00:07:47,855 --> 00:07:50,350
GitLab is very
similar to GitHub,

179
00:07:50,350 --> 00:07:53,110
but it's an open-source
alternative in that you

180
00:07:53,110 --> 00:07:55,930
can host it yourself
on your own server,

181
00:07:55,930 --> 00:07:58,840
inside your corporate
environment, for instance.

182
00:07:58,840 --> 00:08:00,850
It's really like GitHub,

183
00:08:00,850 --> 00:08:03,625
but on a self-hosted
open source platform.

184
00:08:03,625 --> 00:08:07,460
You can also use the hosted
version at gitlab.com.

185
00:08:07,460 --> 00:08:12,760
GitLab has really functional
and useful CI framework

186
00:08:12,760 --> 00:08:14,495
called GitLab CI that's

187
00:08:14,495 --> 00:08:15,940
very similar to Travis-ci

188
00:08:15,940 --> 00:08:17,650
in terms of configuration and it

189
00:08:17,650 --> 00:08:19,240
uses this Yml syntax

190
00:08:19,240 --> 00:08:21,895
for configuration in
setting up the build.

191
00:08:21,895 --> 00:08:24,880
It's also completely
integrated with Gitlab,

192
00:08:24,880 --> 00:08:27,010
rather than having to set
something up like you

193
00:08:27,010 --> 00:08:29,065
do with Travis-ci for GitHub,

194
00:08:29,065 --> 00:08:31,675
this is all just
completely integrated and

195
00:08:31,675 --> 00:08:34,225
after you do the commit,

196
00:08:34,225 --> 00:08:35,665
if you just turn it on,

197
00:08:35,665 --> 00:08:38,970
you'll automatically get reports

198
00:08:38,970 --> 00:08:44,365
and the email and dashboard,

199
00:08:44,365 --> 00:08:45,820
the same one that's
used for development,

200
00:08:45,820 --> 00:08:47,890
you'll see the exact
same information

201
00:08:47,890 --> 00:08:52,070
about the continuous
integration incorporated.

202
00:08:52,070 --> 00:08:55,125
The other really interesting
and useful feature,

203
00:08:55,125 --> 00:08:57,700
especially for embedded
development from GitLab CI,

204
00:08:57,700 --> 00:08:59,740
is that you can
self-host the GitLab

205
00:08:59,740 --> 00:09:02,200
Runner and Cloud host
to GitLab server.

206
00:09:02,200 --> 00:09:03,695
In other words, you could use

207
00:09:03,695 --> 00:09:07,300
the GitLab server in the
Cloud for all your commits,

208
00:09:07,300 --> 00:09:11,500
but then in either some
cases or in all cases,

209
00:09:11,500 --> 00:09:13,745
you can run a runner inside

210
00:09:13,745 --> 00:09:17,200
your corporate environment
that might be connected to

211
00:09:17,200 --> 00:09:20,690
a physical device and
running an automated test on

212
00:09:20,690 --> 00:09:24,830
a physical device as a part
of the validation sequence.

213
00:09:24,830 --> 00:09:27,010
It just gives you some
additional flexibility

214
00:09:27,010 --> 00:09:30,360
about how you would deploy
the automated test runner.

215
00:09:30,360 --> 00:09:32,470
Here's an example of

216
00:09:32,470 --> 00:09:35,755
the GitLab CI implementation
we use for AESD.

217
00:09:35,755 --> 00:09:39,395
This is an example of
a simple yml file that

218
00:09:39,395 --> 00:09:43,585
sets up a Yocto build
and automated test run.

219
00:09:43,585 --> 00:09:45,580
What I'm specifying here are

220
00:09:45,580 --> 00:09:48,300
the docker and docker instances.

221
00:09:48,300 --> 00:09:50,815
What this allows us to do is run

222
00:09:50,815 --> 00:09:53,300
a docker container inside

223
00:09:53,300 --> 00:09:57,080
a docker container
deployed by GitLab CI.

224
00:09:57,080 --> 00:09:58,650
In that docker container,

225
00:09:58,650 --> 00:10:00,280
we can have all the
components that we

226
00:10:00,280 --> 00:10:02,710
need to build and
run a Yocto build.

227
00:10:02,710 --> 00:10:06,040
Where there might be some
flexibility limitations

228
00:10:06,040 --> 00:10:07,910
in what you can do
with Travis-ci,

229
00:10:07,910 --> 00:10:09,730
here you can define
your own container

230
00:10:09,730 --> 00:10:11,110
and do really whatever you want

231
00:10:11,110 --> 00:10:12,250
inside it and have

232
00:10:12,250 --> 00:10:15,865
any components that you
want necessary for Build.

233
00:10:15,865 --> 00:10:20,620
As I mentioned, in addition
to specify how you test and

234
00:10:20,620 --> 00:10:25,730
how the test process actually
works from the yml file,

235
00:10:25,730 --> 00:10:29,120
you can also use a runner
that you define yourself.

236
00:10:30,210 --> 00:10:33,170
If you go into the project
configuration here,

237
00:10:33,170 --> 00:10:37,790
I've actually created a
separate Build Server

238
00:10:37,790 --> 00:10:43,350
that's a physical server
located in the CU environment

239
00:10:43,350 --> 00:10:49,735
that is interacting
with Gitlab web server

240
00:10:49,735 --> 00:10:53,080
and getting notified
whenever there's a new check

241
00:10:53,080 --> 00:10:54,670
in on this repository and

242
00:10:54,670 --> 00:10:56,660
then pulling in the
repository contents,

243
00:10:56,660 --> 00:11:00,220
running the bill based
on this script step,

244
00:11:00,220 --> 00:11:02,740
taking the result output from

245
00:11:02,740 --> 00:11:05,225
the test log and saving
that backup into

246
00:11:05,225 --> 00:11:10,295
the web server from

247
00:11:10,295 --> 00:11:15,130
Gitlab and then populating

248
00:11:15,130 --> 00:11:17,400
the status so that you
can see the status on

249
00:11:17,400 --> 00:11:20,435
Gitlab.com of the build success.

250
00:11:20,435 --> 00:11:23,560
In this case you can see
that the last commit we did,

251
00:11:23,560 --> 00:11:25,775
there's a green checkbox
saying that there was

252
00:11:25,775 --> 00:11:27,970
a passed state on
your build and you

253
00:11:27,970 --> 00:11:30,520
automatically get emails
if your build goes

254
00:11:30,520 --> 00:11:34,280
from failed to success
state or vice versa.